.Dd 2025-09-30
.Dt OSSL_TRACE_ENABLED 3ossl
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm OSSL_trace_enabled ,
.Nm OSSL_trace_begin ,
.Nm OSSL_trace_end ,
.Nm OSSL_TRACE_BEGIN ,
.Nm OSSL_TRACE_END ,
.Nm OSSL_TRACE_CANCEL ,
.Nm OSSL_TRACE ,
.Nm OSSL_TRACE1 ,
.Nm OSSL_TRACE2 ,
.Nm OSSL_TRACE3 ,
.Nm OSSL_TRACE4 ,
.Nm OSSL_TRACE5 ,
.Nm OSSL_TRACE6 ,
.Nm OSSL_TRACE7 ,
.Nm OSSL_TRACE8 ,
.Nm OSSL_TRACE9 ,
.Nm OSSL_TRACEV ,
.Nm OSSL_TRACE_STRING ,
.Nm OSSL_TRACE_STRING_MAX ,
.Nm OSSL_trace_string ,
.Nm OSSL_TRACE_ENABLED
.Nd OpenSSL Tracing API
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In openssl/trace.h
.Ft int
.Fo OSSL_trace_enabled
.Fa "int category"
.Fc
.Ft BIO *
.Fo OSSL_trace_begin
.Fa "int category"
.Fc
.Ft void
.Fo OSSL_trace_end
.Fa "int category"
.Fa "BIO *channel"
.Fc
.Ft /* trace group macros */

.Fo OSSL_TRACE_BEGIN
.Fa category
.Fc
 {
.Ft ...
     if
.Fo 
.Fa some_error
.Fc
 {
.Ft /* Leave trace group prematurely in case of an error */
        
.Fo OSSL_TRACE_CANCEL
.Fa category
.Fc
.Ft goto err;
     }
     ...
 }
.Fo OSSL_TRACE_END
.Fa category
.Fc
.Ft /* one-shot trace macros */

.Fo OSSL_TRACE
.Fa category
.Fa text
.Fc
.Bd -literal
 OSSL_TRACE1(category, format, arg1)
 OSSL_TRACE2(category, format, arg1, arg2)
 ...
 OSSL_TRACE9(category, format, arg1, ..., arg9)
 OSSL_TRACE_STRING(category, text, full, data, len)
.Ed
.Fd #define OSSL_TRACE_STRING_MAX 80
.Ft int
.Fo OSSL_trace_string
.Fa "BIO *out"
.Fa "int text"
.Fa "int full"
.Fa "const unsigned char *data"
.Fa "size_t size"
.Fc
.Ft /* check whether a trace category is enabled */
 if
.Fo 
.Fa OSSL_TRACE_ENABLED(category)
.Fc
 {
.Bd -literal
     ...
 }
.Ed
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The functions described here are mainly interesting for those who
provide OpenSSL functionality, either in OpenSSL itself or in engine
modules or similar.
.Pp
If the tracing facility is enabled (see
.Sx Configure Tracing
below), these functions are used to generate free text tracing output.
.Pp
The tracing output is divided into types which are enabled individually
by the application.
The tracing types are described in detail in
.Xr OSSL_trace_set_callback 3 .
The fallback type
.Sy OSSL_TRACE_CATEGORY_ALL
should
.Em not
be used with the functions described here.
.Pp
Tracing for a specific category is enabled at run-time if a so-called
.Em trace channel
is attached to it.
A trace channel is simply a BIO object to which the application can
write its trace output.
.Pp
The application has two different ways of registering a trace channel,
either by directly providing a BIO object using
.Xr OSSL_trace_set_channel 3 ,
or by providing a callback routine using
.Xr OSSL_trace_set_callback 3 .
The latter is wrapped internally by a dedicated BIO object, so for the
tracing code both channel types are effectively indistinguishable.
We call them a
.Em simple trace channel
and a
.Em callback trace channel ,
respectively.
.Pp
To produce trace output, it is necessary to obtain a pointer to the
trace channel (i.e., the BIO object) using
.Fn OSSL_trace_begin ,
write to it using arbitrary BIO output routines, and finally releases
the channel using
.Fn OSSL_trace_end .
The
.Xr OSSL_trace_begin()/OSSL_trace_end 3
calls surrounding the trace output create a group, which acts as a
critical section (guarded by a mutex) to ensure that the trace output of
different threads does not get mixed up.
.Pp
The tracing code normally does not call
.Xr OSSL_trace_{begin,end} 3
directly, but rather uses a set of convenience macros, see the
.Sx Macros
section below.
.Ss Functions
.Fn OSSL_trace_enabled
can be used to check if tracing for the given
.Vt category
is enabled, i.e., if the tracing facility has been statically enabled
(see
.Sx Configure Tracing
below) and a trace channel has been registered using
.Xr OSSL_trace_set_channel 3
or
.Xr OSSL_trace_set_callback 3 .
.Pp
.Fn OSSL_trace_begin
is used to start a tracing section, and get the channel for the given
.Vt category
in form of a BIO. This BIO can only be used for output.
The pointer returned is NULL if the category is invalid or not enabled.
.Pp
.Fn OSSL_trace_end
is used to end a tracing section.
.Pp
Using
.Fn OSSL_trace_begin
and
.Fn OSSL_trace_end
to wrap tracing sections is
.Em mandatory .
The result of trying to produce tracing output outside of such sections
is undefined.
.Pp
.Fn OSSL_trace_string
outputs
.Fa data
of length
.Fa size
as a string on BIO
.Fa out .
If
.Fa text
is 0, the function masks any included control characters apart from
newlines and makes sure for nonempty input that the output ends with a
newline.
Unless
.Fa full
is nonzero, the length is limited (with a suitable warning) to
.Sy OSSL_TRACE_STRING_MAX
characters, which currently is 80.
.Ss Macros
There are a number of convenience macros defined, to make tracing easy
and consistent.
.Pp
.Fn OSSL_TRACE_BEGIN
and
.Fn OSSL_TRACE_END
reserve the
.Vt BIO
.Qo Li trc_out Qc
and are used as follows to wrap a trace section:
.Bd -literal
 OSSL_TRACE_BEGIN(TLS) {

     BIO_printf(trc_out, ... );

 } OSSL_TRACE_END(TLS);
.Ed
.Pp
This will normally expand to:
.Bd -literal
 do {
     BIO *trc_out = OSSL_trace_begin(OSSL_TRACE_CATEGORY_TLS);
     if (trc_out != NULL) {
         ...
         BIO_printf(trc_out, ...);
     }
     OSSL_trace_end(OSSL_TRACE_CATEGORY_TLS, trc_out);
 } while (0);
.Ed
.Pp
.Fn OSSL_TRACE_CANCEL
must be used before returning from or jumping out of a trace section:
.Bd -literal
 OSSL_TRACE_BEGIN(TLS) {

     if (some_error) {
         OSSL_TRACE_CANCEL(TLS);
         goto err;
     }
     BIO_printf(trc_out, ... );

 } OSSL_TRACE_END(TLS);
.Ed
.Pp
This will normally expand to:
.Bd -literal
 do {
     BIO *trc_out = OSSL_trace_begin(OSSL_TRACE_CATEGORY_TLS);
     if (trc_out != NULL) {
         if (some_error) {
             OSSL_trace_end(OSSL_TRACE_CATEGORY_TLS, trc_out);
             goto err;
         }
         BIO_printf(trc_out, ... );
     }
     OSSL_trace_end(OSSL_TRACE_CATEGORY_TLS, trc_out);
 } while (0);
.Ed
.Pp
.Fn OSSL_TRACE
and
.Xr OSSL_TRACE1 3 ,
.Xr OSSL_TRACE2 3 ,
\&...
.Xr OSSL_TRACE9 3
are so-called one-shot macros:
.Pp
The macro call
.Qo Li OSSL_TRACE(category, text) Qc ,
produces literal text trace output.
.Pp
The macro call
.Qo Li OSSL_TRACEn(category, format, arg1, ..., argn) Qc
produces printf-style trace output with n format field arguments
(n=1,...,9). It expands to:
.Bd -literal
 OSSL_TRACE_BEGIN(category) {
     BIO_printf(trc_out, format, arg1, ..., argN);
 } OSSL_TRACE_END(category)
.Ed
.Pp
Internally, all one-shot macros are implemented using a generic
.Xr OSSL_TRACEV 3
macro, since C90 does not support variadic macros.
This helper macro has a rather weird synopsis and should not be used
directly.
.Pp
The macro call
.Qo Li OSSL_TRACE_STRING(category, text, full, data, len) Qc
outputs
.Fa data
of length
.Fa size
as a string if tracing for the given
.Vt category
is enabled.
It expands to:
.Bd -literal
 OSSL_TRACE_BEGIN(category) {
     OSSL_trace_string(trc_out, text, full, data, len);
 } OSSL_TRACE_END(category)
.Ed
.Pp
The OSSL_TRACE_ENABLED() macro can be used to conditionally execute some
code only if a specific trace category is enabled.
In some situations this is simpler than entering a trace section using
.Fn OSSL_TRACE_BEGIN
and
.Fn OSSL_TRACE_END .
For example, the code
.Bd -literal
 if (OSSL_TRACE_ENABLED(TLS)) {
     ...
 }
.Ed
.Pp
expands to
.Bd -literal
 if (OSSL_trace_enabled(OSSL_TRACE_CATEGORY_TLS) {
     ...
 }
.Ed
.Sh NOTES
It is not needed to guard trace output function calls like
.Em OSSL_TRACE(category, ...)
by
.Em OSSL_TRACE_ENABLED(category) .
.Pp
If producing the trace output requires carrying out auxiliary
calculations, this auxiliary code should be placed inside a conditional
block which is executed only if the trace category is enabled.
.Pp
The most natural way to do this is to place the code inside the trace
section itself because it already introduces such a conditional block.
.Bd -literal
 OSSL_TRACE_BEGIN(TLS) {
     int var = do_some_auxiliary_calculation();

     BIO_printf(trc_out, "var = %d\en", var);

 } OSSL_TRACE_END(TLS);
.Ed
.Pp
In some cases it is more advantageous to use a simple conditional group
instead of a trace section.
This is the case if calculations and tracing happen in different
locations of the code, or if the calculations are so time consuming that
placing them inside a (critical) trace section would create too much
contention.
.Bd -literal
 if (OSSL_TRACE_ENABLED(TLS)) {
     int var = do_some_auxiliary_calculation();

     OSSL_TRACE1("var = %d\en", var);
 }
.Ed
.Pp
Note however that premature optimization of tracing code is in general
futile and it's better to keep the tracing code as simple as possible.
Because most often the limiting factor for the application's speed is
the time it takes to print the trace output, not to calculate it.
.Ss Configure Tracing
By default, the OpenSSL library is built with tracing disabled.
To use the tracing functionality documented here, it is therefore
necessary to configure and build OpenSSL with the 'enable-trace' option.
.Pp
When the library is built with tracing disabled:
.Bl -bullet
.It
The macro
.Sy OPENSSL_NO_TRACE
is defined in
.Pa <openssl/opensslconf.h> .
.It
all functions are still present, but
.Fn OSSL_trace_enabled
will always report the categories as disabled, and all other functions
will do nothing.
.It
the convenience macros are defined to produce dead code.
For example, take this example from
.Sx Macros
section above:
.Bd -literal
 OSSL_TRACE_BEGIN(TLS) {

     if (condition) {
         OSSL_TRACE_CANCEL(TLS);
         goto err;
     }
     BIO_printf(trc_out, ... );

 } OSSL_TRACE_END(TLS);
.Ed
.Pp
When the tracing API isn't operational, that will expand to:
.Bd -literal
 do {
     BIO *trc_out = NULL;
     if (0) {
         if (condition) {
             ((void)0);
             goto err;
         }
         BIO_printf(trc_out, ... );
     }
 } while (0);
.Ed
.El
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn OSSL_trace_enabled
returns 1 if tracing for the given
.Em type
is operational and enabled, otherwise 0.
.Pp
.Fn OSSL_trace_begin
returns a
.Vt BIO
pointer if the given
.Em type
is enabled, otherwise NULL.
.Pp
.Fn OSSL_trace_string
returns the number of characters emitted, or -1 on error.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr OSSL_trace_set_channel 3 ,
.Xr OSSL_trace_set_callback 3
.Sh HISTORY
The OpenSSL Tracing API was added in OpenSSL 3.0.
.Pp
.Xr OSSL_TRACE_STRING 3 ,
.Dv OSSL_TRACE_STRING_MAX ,
and OSSL_trace_string were added in OpenSSL 3.2.
